artifact.self_contained_problem:
For a function f: R^2 → R, define the classical Radon transform by
   (Rf)(α, p) = ∫_{ℓ_{α,p}} f(x) ds(x),
where α ∈ S^1 is a unit vector, p ∈ R, ℓ_{α,p} = { x ∈ R^2 : α · x = p } is the line with unit normal α at signed distance p from the origin, and ds is the element of arclength on this line.

Assume that:
(i) f is continuous on R^2;
(ii) for every α ∈ S^1 and every p ∈ R, the restriction f|_{ℓ_{α,p}} belongs to L^1(ℓ_{α,p}), so that (Rf)(α, p) is well defined;
(iii) there exist constants c > 0 and m ≥ 0 such that |f(x)| ≤ c(1 + |x|^m) for all x ∈ R^2;
(iv) (Rf)(α, p) = 0 for every unit vector α and every real number p.

Does it follow that f ≡ 0 on R^2? More generally, determine the weakest natural growth restriction at infinity on a continuous function f with f|_{ℓ_{α,p}} ∈ L^1 for all (α, p) such that Rf ≡ 0 forces f ≡ 0.

